## General Rules

- Never create a file longer than 500 lines of code. If a file approaches this limit, refactor by splitting it into modules or helper files.
- Always extract any UI element that repeats or could be reused (buttons, input fields, cards, forms, ...) into a separate component file. Do not implement detailed UI or logic directly inside page files; pages should only import and compose these components.
- Always use the `@components/CustomButton.tsx` component whenever a button is needed and customize it with props to achieve the desired results.
- Organize code into clearly separated modules, grouped by feature or responsibility.
- Always define TypeScript types in the `types` folder at the project root. Place generic, widely used types in `types/type.d.ts`, and create separate files (e.g. `driver.d.ts`, `products.d.ts`) for domain-specific types. Never define types inline in components or pages.

## Database access & API routes

- Use the Supabase **server-side** client (`lib/supabase/server.ts`) inside every route handler (`await createClient()`). Never import the browser client on the server.
- Wrap all handlers with the HOFs in `lib/auth/api-auth.ts`:
  • `withAuth()` ⇒ ensures the caller is an authenticated admin/superuser.
  • `withPermission(requiredPermissions, handler)` ⇒ enforces fine-grained RBAC. Export the wrapped function as `GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`, etc.
- Folder structure lives under `app/api/protected/**`:
  • `store/<resource>/route.ts` → list & create operations.
  • `store/<resource>/[id]/route.ts` → read, update & delete a single item.
  • `bulk/route.ts` → batch inserts/updates.
- Follow PostgREST patterns:
  • `insert(...).select().single()` returns the newly created row.  
  • Use joins (`select("*, relation(*)")`) instead of multiple round-trips when possible.
- Prefer **soft deletion** (set `soft_deleted = true`) over hard deletes and always filter reads with `.eq("soft_deleted", false)`.
- Validate path params & body early. Respond with JSON and proper status codes: 400 (bad input), 401 (unauthenticated), 403 (forbidden), 404 (not found), 409 (conflict), 500 (server error).
- Use a temporary **service-role** client only for privileged tasks such as storage uploads (`SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY`); never expose this key to the browser.
- Keep route files short (<120 LOC). Extract complex validation or business logic into helpers in `app/api/_lib/**` when needed.
- Front-end pages/components call these endpoints via `fetch("/api/protected/...")`; they must not query the database directly.
- Log `error.code` from Supabase/Postgres to surface constraint violations (e.g., `23505` ⇒ 409 Conflict).

## Global state

- Manage shared application state via **React Context** providers located in `providers/` (see `providers/user-provider.tsx` for a reference implementation).
- Each provider must:
  • Declare a strict TypeScript interface describing the context value.
  • Export both the provider component **and** a `useContext()` hook that throws if used outside its provider.
  • Keep state minimal and focused on a single responsibility/domain.
- Handle side-effects (API calls, Supabase listeners, websockets) **inside** the provider so consuming components stay pure/stateless.
- Expose imperative helpers (e.g., `refreshUser`) through the context instead of prop-drilling callbacks.
- Mount all global providers at the root (`app/layout.tsx`) so their state is available throughout the app.
- Use component-local state for transient UI concerns (open/close flags, form drafts); avoid polluting global state.
- Reach for a lightweight store (e.g., **Zustand**) only when:
  • Multiple otherwise-unrelated providers would clutter the tree, **or**
  • You need to share state outside React (event handlers, non-component files).
  Document the rationale in code comments before introducing it.
- **Never** store sensitive data (access tokens, secrets) in client-side global state.

